  "basics": true,
  "order": 2,
  "title": "Azure RBAC: role assignments and scope inheritance",
  "summary": "A role assignment answers three questions: who (a principal), what (a role definition) and where (a scope). Assignments flow down the scope tree, from management group to subscription to resource group to resource.",
  "notes": [
    "Scopes nest: management group > subscription > resource group > resource. An assignment at one level applies to everything below it.",
    "Assign roles to groups, not to individuals. Access then follows group membership, and reviews cover one list instead of many assignments.",
    "Control plane and data plane are separate. Contributor can manage a storage account but can't read its blobs; that needs a data role such as Storage Blob Data Reader.",
    "Access is the union of all assignments that apply; there is no 'least permissive wins'. Deny assignments exist, but only managed features such as deployment stacks create them.",
    "Entra roles (e.g. User Administrator) are a different system: they control the directory, not Azure resources. Use PIM for privileged roles in both."
  ],
